# Persistence Adapters

This tutorial starts a Jido instance with a persistence adapter, saves an Agent, restores it, and then moves to a different adapter without changing the Agent. It runs with no external service.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "migration/consolidated-v3"}
])
```

## Define an Agent

The Agent knows nothing about storage. It holds a paid amount and adds to it on each payment Signal.

```elixir
defmodule AdaptersGuide.ApplyPayment do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "adapters_guide_apply_payment",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{cents: Jido.Joi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{cents: cents}, %{agent_state: state}),
    do: {:ok, %{state | paid_cents: state.paid_cents + cents}}
end

defmodule AdaptersGuide.Invoice do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "adapters_guide_invoice"

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{paid_cents: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0)})
  end

  routes do
    route "invoice.pay", AdaptersGuide.ApplyPayment
  end
end

payment = Jido.Signal.new!("invoice.pay", %{cents: 250}, source: "/billing")
payment.type
```

The cell returns `"invoice.pay"`.

## Check an adapter declaration

An adapter declaration is a module or a `{module, options}` tuple. `Jido.Persistence.resolve_config/2` checks the declaration without starting anything. It requires the `get/2` and `compare_and_swap/4` callbacks and runs the adapter's option validation.

```elixir
results = %{
  ets: Jido.Persistence.resolve_config({Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: :adapters_guide}, nil),
  file_without_path: Jido.Persistence.resolve_config(Jido.Persistence.File, nil),
  disabled: Jido.Persistence.resolve_config(false, nil)
}

{:ok, {Jido.Persistence.ETS, [table: :adapters_guide]}} = results.ets

{:error, {:invalid_persistence_options, Jido.Persistence.File, ":path must be a non-empty string"}} =
  results.file_without_path

{:ok, nil} = results.disabled

results
```

The file adapter needs `:path`, and `false` or `nil` disables persistence. A successful result does not prove the storage is durable or shared across nodes.

## Start an instance with ETS

Configure the adapter on the instance. A persistent Agent also needs a stable namespace, because the namespace is part of its durable key.

```elixir
{:ok, _instance} =
  Jido.start_link(
    name: AdaptersGuide.EtsJido,
    namespace: "billing",
    persistence: {Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: :adapters_guide}
  )

{:ok, invoice} =
  Jido.start_agent(AdaptersGuide.EtsJido, AdaptersGuide.Invoice,
    id: "inv-1001",
    persistence: :inherit
  )

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(invoice)

{:ok, agent, _directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(invoice, payment)
snapshot = Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(invoice)

{agent.state, snapshot.state_version}
```

The cell returns `{%{paid_cents: 250}, 1}`. Revision zero was written before `await_ready/1` returned. The successful Turn wrote revision one.

## Restore from storage

Read the record, hibernate the Agent, and thaw it. `Jido.thaw/4` starts a new process that requires the saved record.

```elixir
{:ok, stored, 1} =
  Jido.Persistence.load_agent_with_revision(
    AdaptersGuide.EtsJido,
    AdaptersGuide.Invoice,
    "inv-1001"
  )

:ok = Jido.hibernate(AdaptersGuide.EtsJido, invoice)

{:ok, thawed} = Jido.thaw(AdaptersGuide.EtsJido, AdaptersGuide.Invoice, "inv-1001")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(thawed)

{stored.state, Jido.AgentServer.agent(thawed).state, Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(thawed).state_version}
```

The cell returns `{%{paid_cents: 250}, %{paid_cents: 250}, 1}`.

Passing the instance name as the source makes `Jido.Persistence` use that instance's adapter and namespace. You can also pass `{adapter, options}` with `:namespace` to read the same record without an instance.

## Stop the instance

Stop the Agent and the instance. The next section starts a new instance with the same namespace.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(AdaptersGuide.EtsJido, thawed)
Supervisor.stop(AdaptersGuide.EtsJido)
```

ETS data survives a stopped instance because a supervised process in the Jido application inherits the table. It does not survive a BEAM restart.

## Swap to files

Change the declaration and nothing else. The Agent and its Signals stay the same.

```elixir
path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "adapters-guide-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}")
file_store = {Jido.Persistence.File, path: path}

{:ok, _instance} =
  Jido.start_link(name: AdaptersGuide.FileJido, namespace: "billing", persistence: file_store)

{:ok, invoice} =
  Jido.start_agent(AdaptersGuide.FileJido, AdaptersGuide.Invoice,
    id: "inv-2001",
    persistence: :inherit
  )

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(invoice)
{:ok, _agent, _directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(invoice, payment)
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(AdaptersGuide.FileJido, invoice)
Supervisor.stop(AdaptersGuide.FileJido)

path |> Path.join("records") |> File.ls!() |> length()
```

The cell returns `1`, the one file for this Agent. Its name is a hash of the storage key.

The instance is gone, but the record remains on disk. A new instance with the same namespace can read it:

```elixir
{:ok, _instance} =
  Jido.start_link(name: AdaptersGuide.RestartedJido, namespace: "billing", persistence: file_store)

{:ok, restored} = Jido.thaw(AdaptersGuide.RestartedJido, AdaptersGuide.Invoice, "inv-2001")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(restored)
state = Jido.AgentServer.agent(restored).state

:ok = Jido.stop_agent(AdaptersGuide.RestartedJido, restored)
Supervisor.stop(AdaptersGuide.RestartedJido)
File.rm_rf!(path)

state
```

The cell returns `%{paid_cents: 250}`. The instance name changed and the record still loaded, because the key uses the namespace.

## Override per Agent

`:persistence` on `Jido.start_agent/3` also accepts a declaration, which replaces the instance default for that Agent. The Agent still takes its namespace from the instance.

```elixir
{:ok, _instance} =
  Jido.start_link(name: AdaptersGuide.MixedJido, namespace: "billing")

{:ok, invoice} =
  Jido.start_agent(AdaptersGuide.MixedJido, AdaptersGuide.Invoice,
    id: "inv-3001",
    persistence: {Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: :adapters_guide_override}
  )

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(invoice)

{:ok, %{id: "inv-3001"}} =
  Jido.Persistence.load_agent(
    {Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: :adapters_guide_override},
    AdaptersGuide.Invoice,
    "inv-3001",
    namespace: "billing"
  )

:ok = Jido.stop_agent(AdaptersGuide.MixedJido, invoice)
Supervisor.stop(AdaptersGuide.MixedJido)
```

The cell returns `:ok`.

## Choose an adapter

Jido ships these adapters. All of them implement `Jido.Persistence.Store`.

| Adapter | Storage | Survives a BEAM restart | Shared across BEAM instances | Extra dependency | Guide |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Jido.Persistence.ETS` | Memory | No | No | None | [Local Stores](local-persistence-adapters.md) |
| `Jido.Persistence.File` | One file per key | Yes | No. One BEAM must own the directory | None | [Local Stores](local-persistence-adapters.md) |
| `Jido.Persistence.Mnesia` | An application-owned Mnesia table | Depends on your table copies | Depends on your table copies | `:mnesia` in `extra_applications` | [Local Stores](local-persistence-adapters.md) |
| `Jido.Persistence.Ecto` | One SQL table on PostgreSQL or SQLite | Yes | Yes on PostgreSQL | `:ecto_sql` and a driver | [Ecto Storage](ecto-persistence.md) |
| `Jido.Persistence.Redis` | Redis keys, through your client function | Depends on your Redis setup | Yes | A Redis client | [Redis and S3 Stores](redis-and-s3-persistence.md) |
| `Jido.Persistence.S3` | One object per key, through your request function | Yes | Yes | An S3 client | [Redis and S3 Stores](redis-and-s3-persistence.md) |
| `Jido.Persistence.Bedrock` | A Bedrock cluster | Depends on your cluster's durability profile | Yes | `:bedrock` and `:bedrock_raft` | [Bedrock and Raft Storage](bedrock-persistence.md) |

For anything else, see [Custom Adapters](custom-persistence-adapter.md). No adapter gives you exclusive ownership of an Agent across a cluster. Revision checks reject stale writes, and they do not elect a single owner.

